<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>
    <RootNamespace>EmberTern.LicenseManager</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>EmberTern.LicenseManager</AssemblyName>
    <!-- ⭐⭐ THE ONE IDENTITY VALUE THIS PROJECT OVERRIDES, and the reason is that the inherited one was
         simply wrong: `Directory.Build.props` declares `<Product>EmberTern</Product>`, so until L9 this
         executable claimed its product was EmberTern — in the Windows file properties, in the EXE's
         resources, and in anything reading the assembly back. It is not EmberTern; it is the tool that
         issues EmberTern's licences.

         ⛔ Nothing ELSE is overridden here. Version, ReleaseDate, Company and Copyright stay inherited
         from that one PropertyGroup, which is what makes `ManagerInfo` and `AppInfo` structurally unable
         to disagree about which release this is. ⛔ And still never a version number in code. -->
    <Product>EmberTern License Manager</Product>
    <!-- ⭐ The OS icon of the executable — EmberTern's own .ico, referenced across the project
         boundary rather than copied. Same file, same brand, one master. This is what puts the icon
         in Explorer, in the EXE properties and on the taskbar button. -->
    <ApplicationIcon>..\EmberTern.App\Assets\Branding\EmberTern.ico</ApplicationIcon>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ⭐⭐ THE TOKEN LAYER IS LINKED FROM EmberTern.App, NOT COPIED.

       One source of every colour and every metric, for two applications. A copy would drift on the
       first palette change, and the drift would be invisible until someone put the two windows side
       by side.

       ⚠⚠ MEASURED CORRECTION TO THE DESIGN (docs/design/licensing-system.md §12.1 said "link
       Themes/*.axaml"). Only these FOUR files are linkable. The rest of EmberTern's theme layer binds
       to types this application does not have and must not acquire:

         IconGeometries.axaml     → ControlTheme for controls:SvgIcon / DebuggerIcon / CreateIcon
         ControlThemes.axaml      → the CheckBox template instantiates controls:SvgIcon
         SearchableComboBox.axaml → controls:
         PickerTemplates.axaml    → EmberTern.App + EmberTern.Core.Metadata
         ControlStyles.axaml      → all of the above, plus AvaloniaEdit, plus DataGrid, plus
                                    avares://EmberTern/Assets/Branding/EmberTern.ico

       So the License Manager brings its own Themes/LicenseManagerStyles.axaml — small, and allowed to
       contain STRUCTURE and METRICS only. ⛔ It may not define a single colour: every brush it paints
       with comes from the linked Colors.axaml. LicenseManagerThemeTests fails the build otherwise,
       which is what keeps "two style files" from becoming "two palettes". -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Themes\Colors.axaml" Link="Themes\Colors.axaml" />
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Themes\Tokens.axaml" Link="Themes\Tokens.axaml" />
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Themes\Typography.axaml" Link="Themes\Typography.axaml" />
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Themes\FluentBridge.axaml" Link="Themes\FluentBridge.axaml" />
    <!-- ⭐⭐ THE DATAGRID STANDARD — linkable since the 2026-08-18 split, and that split existed for this.
         Row height, header height, cell padding, zebra striping, selection-over-zebra, hover, the column
         header's chrome and the cell focus ring: EmberTern's ONE grid appearance, in a file that binds to
         nothing but the tokens above it. It used to sit inside `ControlStyles.axaml`, which is unlinkable
         for the reasons listed below, so the licences list here would otherwise have been a lookalike
         maintained twice.
         ⛔ Do not copy a grid metric into this project. `DataGridStylesSplitTests` (in EmberTern.Tests)
         fails the build if that file stops being portable, and `LicenseManagerThemeTests` if it is copied
         here rather than linked. -->
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Themes\DataGridStyles.axaml"
                      Link="Themes\DataGridStyles.axaml" />
    <!-- ⭐⭐ THE ICON GEOMETRIES — linkable since the 2026-08-16 split, and that split existed for this.
         The file used to end with three ControlThemes bound to controls:SvgIcon / DebuggerIcon /
         CreateIcon; those 164 lines were the ONLY type reference in an otherwise pure catalogue of 86
         <StreamGeometry> resources, and they were what made the whole dictionary unusable here. They now
         live in EmberTern.App/Themes/IconControlThemes.axaml, which this project deliberately does NOT
         link — it has no such controls and must not acquire them.
         ⛔ Do not copy a geometry into this project. One source of every icon, two applications;
         IconGeometriesSplitTests (in EmberTern.Tests) fails the build if the file stops being pure. -->
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Themes\IconGeometries.axaml"
                      Link="Themes\IconGeometries.axaml" />
    <!-- ⚠ Only the LINKED files are listed. The Avalonia SDK already includes every .axaml under this
         project directory, so naming App.axaml, Views\**\*.axaml or the local Themes file here produces
         AVLN2002 "duplicate x:Class" rather than the belt-and-braces it looks like. -->

    <!-- ⭐ THE WINDOW ICON — the same file as ApplicationIcon above, linked so that Avalonia can resolve
         it as avares://EmberTern.LicenseManager/Assets/Branding/EmberTern.ico. ⛔ It is NOT copied into
         this project: one master, two applications, exactly as with the token layer. The two references
         answer different questions — ApplicationIcon is a Win32 resource compiled into the EXE (Explorer,
         file properties, the taskbar button), this one is the icon Avalonia paints in the window's own
         title bar and hands to Alt+Tab. Both are needed; neither replaces the other. -->
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Assets\Branding\EmberTern.ico"
                      Link="Assets\Branding\EmberTern.ico" />

    <!-- ⭐ THE UI MARK for the About window (L9) — the 256×256 transparent PNG, LINKED for exactly the
         same reason the .ico above is: one master, two applications. ⛔ Not copied.
         ⚠⚠ It is DIFFERENT artwork from the .ico, from a different master, ON PURPOSE — an icon is judged
         at 16-32 px in dense chrome and this at 128 px on a quiet window. Replacing one does not replace
         the other; `EmberTern.App/Assets/Branding/BRANDING.md` is the authority and lists its consumers.
         ⭐ This reference is the only thing that makes `avares://EmberTern.LicenseManager/Assets/Branding/
         EmberTern_logo.png` resolvable here — the product's own avares path is a different assembly. -->
    <AvaloniaResource Include="..\EmberTern.App\Assets\Branding\EmberTern_logo.png"
                      Link="Assets\Branding\EmberTern_logo.png" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Versions pinned to EmberTern.App's, deliberately. Two Avalonia versions in one repository is a
         maintenance trap, and this repository already carries two documented version mismatches it did
         not choose (docs/design/avalonia-12.1.1-update.md). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.2" />
    <!-- ⚠ NUMER NIE ZGADZA SIĘ Z COREM CELOWO, dokładnie jak w EmberTern.App: DataGrid ma własne repo i
         własny cykl wydań — przy corze 12.1.1 najnowszy DataGrid to 12.1.2, a wydania 12.1.1 nie ma.
         ⛔ Wersja MUSI być ta sama co w EmberTern.App: obie aplikacje dzielą jeden plik stylu siatki
         (`Themes/DataGridStyles.axaml`, linkowany wyżej), a ten styl celuje w NAZWANE części szablonu
         Fluenta (`Rectangle#BackgroundRectangle`, `Grid#PART_ColumnHeaderRoot`). Dwie wersje pakietu to
         dwa szablony i cicha rozbieżność wyglądu, bez żadnego błędu. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid" Version="12.1.2" />
    <!-- The register of record (§18.1). First-party, one package, one file on disk. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!-- ⭐ DPAPI for the SMTP password at rest (L6, decision D‑1). First-party, out-of-band package —
         ProtectedData is NOT in the BCL, exactly as EmberTern.App records at its own reference, and the
         version is pinned EQUAL to App's so the repository carries one DPAPI implementation version.
         ⛔ The only new package L6 adds. MailKit is not needed: the SMTP auth question was measured on
         2026-08-19 and basic auth over STARTTLS works (235 Accepted), so System.Net.Mail suffices.
         ⚠ This package's members are Windows-only and the platform analyzer CA1416 ENFORCES it — under
         TreatWarningsAsErrors an unguarded call is a build error, measured with a negative control.
         Every call site therefore sits behind OperatingSystem.IsWindows(); see LocalDpapiProtector. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ⭐ Same arrangement EmberTern.Firebird already uses. It exists for exactly one reason here:
         BackupWorkflow's snapshot VERIFICATION only fires when a snapshot fails to reproduce the register,
         and that state cannot be reached through the public API — so without a seam the guard has nothing
         watching it and deleting it would leave every test green.
         ⚠ Provenance, stated accurately: the gap was identified by reading the code during L5.5 and
         reported to the user as a known testability limit; it was NOT established by a fault-injection
         run. The L5.5 injection campaign was interrupted before it produced any result and was then
         retired by user directive (gotcha #383 as amended). -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="EmberTern.LicenseManager.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ⭐⭐ THE MESSAGE TEMPLATES — one pair per language, embedded rather than loose on disk.
       A template is part of the build, not something an operator edits: an editable file would be a
       fifth thing to back up and would let a message say something the register cannot reproduce.
       ⛔ There is no `if` anywhere that picks between them — a language code plus a body kind NAME the
       resource (LicenseEmailTemplates), so a third language is two files plus one row in
       MessageLanguages. `LicenseEmailTemplateTests` asserts every expected name is actually embedded,
       because a renamed or unembedded file would otherwise surface as an empty message at send time. -->
  <!-- ⚠⚠⚠ `WithCulture="false"` IS LOAD-BEARING — WITHOUT IT THESE FOUR FILES DO NOT REACH THIS ASSEMBLY
       AT ALL, AND NOTHING REPORTS IT.

       MSBuild reads the segment before the extension as a CULTURE, and `pl` and `en` are both real
       culture names. So `LicenceEmail.pl.html` was classified as a Polish culture-specific resource and
       compiled into a SATELLITE assembly — measured in the build log:

         "Kultura 'pl' została przypisana do pliku 'Email\Templates\LicenceEmail.pl.html'."
         csc … /out:obj\Debug\net9.0\pl\EmberTern.LicenseManager.resources.dll  /resource:…pl.html…

       ⚠⚠ Every surface said it was fine: `msbuild -getItem:EmbeddedResource` listed all four with correct
       paths, the csc command line carried all four `/resource:` switches, and the build reported 0
       warnings / 0 errors. The main assembly simply carried none of them, and `GetManifestResourceNames()`
       returned only Avalonia's `!AvaloniaResources`. At run time that surfaces as an EMPTY MESSAGE BODY,
       not as an error — which is why `LicenseEmailTemplateTests` asserts embedding rather than trusting
       it, and is what caught this on its first run.

       ⭐ `LogicalName` is declared alongside so the name is a DECLARED fact that
       `LicenseEmailTemplates.ResourceName` composes to match, rather than a convention to re-derive.
       ⛔ Do not remove either attribute, and ⛔ do not "simplify" the file names to avoid the problem —
       the language belongs in the name, and that is exactly what makes a third language two files. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Email\Templates\LicenceEmail.pl.html"
                      LogicalName="EmberTern.LicenseManager.Email.Templates.LicenceEmail.pl.html"
                      WithCulture="false" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Email\Templates\LicenceEmail.pl.txt"
                      LogicalName="EmberTern.LicenseManager.Email.Templates.LicenceEmail.pl.txt"
                      WithCulture="false" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Email\Templates\LicenceEmail.en.html"
                      LogicalName="EmberTern.LicenseManager.Email.Templates.LicenceEmail.en.html"
                      WithCulture="false" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Email\Templates\LicenceEmail.en.txt"
                      LogicalName="EmberTern.LicenseManager.Email.Templates.LicenceEmail.en.txt"
                      WithCulture="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EmberTern.Licensing\EmberTern.Licensing.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EmberTern.Licensing.Issuing\EmberTern.Licensing.Issuing.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
